General Knowledge: Who was the first king to make a code of law in human history?
(World): Law is one of the most important elements in governing the country and the world. Without law, many crimes will occur, people will live in insecurity, there is no peace. The previous generation initiated the creation of the Code. Who was the first king in human history to make a code?
King Hammurabi led his army on several occasions to expand his power over the surrounding kingdoms of Babylon. He brought all the Mesopotamian states under his control until a powerful "Babylonian Empire" was formed.
The Hammurabi Code contained 282 articles to help resolve disputes and to prosecute almost all areas at that time. These include laws relating to trade, borrowing, stealing, injuring, and murder. For all these laws, it is also a model, an idea and an example for today's law-making. For actual crimes, there must be actual penalties accordingly.
However, injustice does not mean that it does not exist. Laws sometimes do not apply to higher social classes, and the general practice differs somewhat. For mischievous provocations against the rich or the upper classes, punishment is more severe than for ordinary people.
The following are some of the contents of the Hammurabi Code:
Article 196: If anyone hurts the eyes of others, the perpetrator shall be punished in the same way.
Article 197: Whoever breaks the bones of others, the perpetrator shall be punished in the same manner as the victim.
