Discussion on "Sustainable Development Under China's Belt and Road Initiative in Lancang-Mekong Countries to be Held in Cambodia"
(Phnom Penh): Discussions on "Sustainable Development under China's Belt and Road Initiative in the Lancang-Mekong Country" to be held in Cambodia.
This meeting was held this morning, June 30, 2021 in Phnom Penh with the participation of Mr. Van Phal, Senior Advisor of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, Mr. Ek Sam Ol, President of the Cambodia-China Friendship Association. Leaders from relevant ministries, major companies in Cambodia, Mr. Oknha Liu Yang, President of the Cambodia-China Business Association and Chairman of the Board of Jing Sa Holding Group.
Mr. Van Phal expressed his strong support for joint river management during the past year under the Special Fund for Mekong-Lanchang Cooperation in the Sustainable Development Dialogue under the "Belt and Road Initiative". China's "road" in the country along the Lancang-Mekong River, organized by the International Trade Association of Cambodia.
He said Cambodia, along with Laos, Thailand and Vietnam, which are members of the Mekong River Commission, is committed to maintaining the ecological function of the river basin in line with the 1995 Mekong Agreement on Cooperation for Sustainable Development. Sustainability of the Mekong Basin and recognize that the river basin environment is a shared resource, any activity in one area will have the potential and not affect the other part of the river basin.
"As we all know, the Mekong River Basin is one of the most important biodiversity hotspots in the world," he said.
The source of the Mekong River and the Lancang River is an important part of China-ASEAN cooperation. Although economic development in the region has been slow, Cambodia-China relations, both traditional and traditional, remain cohesive and well-maintained. Prioritize China's development experience in accordance with the strategy of "back wave, front wave" to achieve industrial processing and economic growth, especially in Cambodia.
Article 3 of the agreement stipulates the protection of the environment, natural resources, biological conditions and ecological balance of river basins to avoid pollution or other adverse effects arising from all development and use plans. Water and other related resources in the Mekong Basin.
