The international community continues to provide assistance to Cambodia in the field of mine action in order to end the mine threat by 2025
(Phnom Penh): On behalf of the Royal Government of Cambodia, Senior Minister Ly Thuch, First Vice President of the Mine Action Authority, assessed that the international community continues to support the Royal Government of Cambodia in the field of mine action through mine action projects in order to end the mine threat. 2025.
This statement was made on the morning of June 30, 2021 at the Board Meeting of the Mine Action Project for the 4th Phase through a video system led by Senior Minister Ly Thuch, First Vice President of the Mine Authority. Representatives from the Ministries of Economy and Finance, donors and development partners include: UNDP Australia, the International Cooperation Agency of the Republic of Korea (KOICA) and New Zealand.
The purpose of this meeting was to review the results achieved in the first half of the year and the action plan in the second half of 2021 of the implementation of the demining project for the fourth phase.
* First: Clearing mines and explosive remnants of war * Second: Supporting disability relief activities, education on mine risk and gender mainstreaming * Third: Strengthening national capacity
The senior minister added that by analyzing the results of the implementation of NMAS, the first three-year plan has clearly highlighted the need to increase efforts to mobilize additional resources and establish a technical working group to discuss cleanup and remediation methods. Safer and faster land liberation, the first result of which is the basis for the implementation of our second three-year plan from 2021-2023.
The Australian Representative, Ryan Tierney, First Secretary of the Australian Embassy in Cambodia, expressed support for the inclusion of all activities in the project implementation plan and believed in the mine authority's ability to implement the project until 2025 and coordinate. From UNDP partners, the fourth phase of the demining project will be successful according to the agreed plan.
Ms. JI Yea Kyung, Deputy Country Director of KOICA Cambodia on behalf of the Government of the Republic of Korea, acknowledged that all activities set out in the Mine Action Plan in Cambodia have contributed to the development and support of the project. Focused on priority provinces.
She added that the Government of the Republic of Korea is pleased to be a partner and good friend of the people and the Royal Government of Cambodia for the benefit of the people and the sustainable development of Cambodia. Mines are not just for peace, but to build the foundation for Cambodia's prosperity.
As a result, the meeting agreed as follows:
In addition to donors for this project, other donor countries and development partners have been supporting the Royal Government of Cambodia in demining work in Cambodia, including the United States, the United Kingdom, Japan, Germany, Switzerland, China, Norway, Canada, Ireland, and UNICEF, as well as NGOs. Civilians and philanthropists as well.
