Singaporeans vie for Chinese vaccine and no one asks for US vaccine
After the World Health Organization (WHO) approved the introduction of China's 19-dose Kovid vaccine for immediate use, Singapore took immediate action to identify and approve it, according to foreign media reports. Let 24 private medical institutions become suppliers of the vaccine.
But since the start of the Chinese vaccination, the hotline for vaccinations has been on hold and almost exploded. Medical staff at a hospital said that after being able to get the vaccine, their website received hundreds of messages asking for information each day, all about the vaccine.
Another private medical institution is in a similar situation, with the hospital bustling, a medical staff member said, with at least 800 calls received each day from morning to night. The phone is ringing non-stop. The vaccine is currently being introduced in Singapore but has not reached the supply level.
Reuters interviewed a vaccination site and confirmed that the number of vaccination appointments received was more than a thousand, but there were only 200 doses left in their stockpile, as one person had to be vaccinated twice. Doses, so the vaccine in stock can be used for 100 injections, which is only one tenth of the number of injections.
