(Phnom Penh): The Cloud Tiger is listed on the IUCN Red List as the most endangered species in the world. Many rare species of wildlife have been captured by the Ministry of Environment's research team through the installation of automatic cameras in some protected areas in Cambodia. This is according to Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Environment, on June 29, 2021.
Data collected from automatic cameras deployed in the Cambodia Wildlife Sanctuary captured images of clouded tigers. This data is useful for the management of those protected areas.
According to the Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment, "Clouded Tigers are on the verge of extinction and are listed on the site." "This species is threatened by poaching and trapping, and the loss of habitat for foraging is also a problem that leads to declining wildlife populations."
He said there were no clear data on how many cloud tigers were present in Cambodia at present, but that the population of cloud tigers was in the northeast, southwest and north of Cambodia. According to experts, cloud tigers are found in Bhutan, China, India, Indonesia (Sumatra), Laos, Malaysia, Sarawak, Myanmar, Nepal, Thailand and Vietnam. The tiger has become extinct in Bangladesh, Singapore and Taiwan.
Cloud tigers are easy to notice, we just look at its body has a cloud-like patch scattered throughout the body.
Seven more financial institutions eligible for $ 200 million government-guaranteed financing for businesses
(Phnom Penh): The Credit Guarantee Corporation of Cambodia on June 29, 2021 signed the second round of credit guarantee agreement with participating financial institutions, including Amret Microfinance Institution, Bradbank, Microfinance Institution. Khema, Foreign Trade Bank of Cambodia, Jettrust Royal Bank, LOLC Microfinance Institution and Sathapana Bank.
The partnership with banks and microfinance institutions today will increase the total number of participating financial institutions of the Cambodian Credit Guarantee Corporation to 15. The signing of the agreement allows the Cambodian Credit Guarantee Corporation and financial institutions to work together to provide secured loans to businesses in Cambodia.
The increase in the number of participating financial institutions will make secured financing more comprehensive, competitive, and provide more options and opportunities for businesses to access financing across the country.
The Cambodian Credit Guarantee Corporation provides support to participating financial institutions to provide financing to businesses that are able to repay but lack collateral, providing collateral that acts as collateral between 70% and 80% of the loan.
The presence of the Cambodian Credit Guarantee Corporation makes it possible for businesses to obtain loans from participating financial institutions even if they do not have sufficient collateral.
CGCC launched a $ 200 million business recovery guarantee scheme in March 2021 to support micro, small and medium enterprises and large enterprises to increase access to credit from financial institutions. Participate for working capital, investment and business expansion.
The Business Guarantee Credit Scheme is valid until the end of 2022, and the credit guarantee will be based on the principle of "come first, get first". To date, 18 guarantees have been provided to participating financial institutions under a business recovery credit guarantee scheme.
(Siem Reap): Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Senior Minister of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport, said that in order to achieve its vision for a digital economy, Cambodia has committed to implementing the ASYCUDA customs system, a project to connect the Port EDI Autonomous System with the ASYCUDA system to facilitate Efficient transportation and trade.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol said this on June 29, 2021, on the occasion of leading a delegation of the Ministry to participate in the World Economic Forum via remote video under the theme: "Recovery of trade activities: the promotion. "The regional economy is recovering with a resilient supply chain."
The Customs Data Automation System (ASYCUDA) is a computer system designed by the United Nations Conference on Trade and Development (UNCTAD) to govern a country's customs procedures. The system was introduced and implemented in May 2008 at the Sihanoukville Autonomous Port to enhance trade facilitation and increase Cambodia's competitiveness.
He also stressed the importance of digital systems to reduce the impact of Kovid-19 and to be effective solutions to support public and commercial activities. It can enable ASEAN Member States to work together to promote ASEAN as a single digital connectivity community and promote its connectivity to accelerate the recovery of economic and social development.
