Ministry of Environment spokesman: Plantation plays an important role as exports and use of natural forest timber are restricted globally
(Phnom Penh): "Plantation plantations play an important role in serving the environment and the national economy and the local economy, and investment in plantations has seen an increase, such as acacia, acacia, oil and wind."
This was stated by Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment during a visit on July 26, 2021 at Maisak Plantation, an investment by a company from Israel to plant 350 hectares in the community land of Tasal Nature Reserve. In Oral Wildlife Sanctuary, Oral District, Kampong Speu Province. This teak plantation is growing well and is planned to be harvested in the coming years.
The Secretary of State stated that along with the global restrictions on the export and use of timber extracted from natural forests, the demand for timber from plantations such as acacia, teak, kerosene, windmills, etc. has increased. There has been a significant increase.
At the same time, Mr. Net Pheaktra said that investing in plantations provides many benefits, such as: creating jobs and increasing income for local communities and providing income to the national economy, reducing pressure on the use of natural wood and increasing forest cover. For Cambodia as well.
The senior official added that in general, in local communities, about 30-50% of the total land area is degraded forest land. The Ministry of Environment encourages and promotes the planting of trees on the degraded land of local communities in protected areas as well as other areas to restore forest cover and increase income sources for local communities.
A representative of the teak plantation company from Israel, Mr. Yuthan Gross, said that the company is very honored that the leaders of the Ministry of Environment came to visit the plantation.
He added, "The company is really happy to bring this project here, and here one day it will become a potential commercial area in Cambodia." At the same time, the company hopes to have more cooperation with more communities in Cambodia. This project will be successful and will benefit the country and the people of Cambodia.
Yuthan Gross added that the first year project was very interesting as the company worked closely with the community, with a direct workforce in the area that would provide them with sustainable employment.
Ms. Phun Samnang, a community representative in the Tasal Nature Reserve, Tasal commune, Oral district, Kampong Speu province, said that the planting of teak trees by experts from Israel is growing fast and has provided jobs for the people in this community. She added that if the forest remains in Kong Vong, people can go and find vegetables in the forest to sell to support their families in addition to their income.
