In fact, the Ministry of Commerce recently held an internal meeting to review and discuss the input on the draft national policy on cashews, chaired by Mr. Reach Ra, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Commerce and Deputy Chairman of the Joint Policy Working Group. Cambodian cashew.
The meeting was attended by the Royal Government Delegation in charge of the President, Green Trade Co., Ltd., Acting Director General of Domestic Trade, Deputy Director General of Trade Promotion, Chairman - Acting Chairman of the relevant departments and institutes, and various technical officers of the Trade Policy Department. .
The internal meeting reviewed and discussed the input of the above draft national policy on cashew nuts in order to turn Cambodia into an important producer and supplier of cashew products to serve the local, regional and global market.
According to Mr. Reach Ra, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Commerce, the meeting reviewed the agenda of each content by discussing and providing in-depth input on key issues such as the introduction, vision, goals and objectives and strategies of the national policy. On cashews.
Mr. Reach Ra said that for the work to continue, the department will edit the content agreed in accordance with the spirit of the meeting to submit to the next meeting.
According to Pen Sovicheat, Undersecretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Commerce, the cashew market in Cambodia will not be easily lost and the likelihood of this happening is slim.
Mr. Pen Sovichet stated that the reasons for this argument are based on the existing trade relations and the history of market demand for the quality of Cambodian cashew nuts.
At the same time, he said that in the past, Cambodia exported most of the unprocessed cashew nuts for neighboring countries to buy and process for resale, but now Cambodia has many cashew processing factories and handicrafts that can reach Find processing to serve exports.
A spokesman for the Ministry of Commerce emphasized that Cambodia was looking to export directly to its target markets without going through intermediaries.
Mr. Pen Sovicheat said that the Ministry of Commerce is preparing a policy to promote the trade in cashew products with the participation of other relevant inter-ministerial ministries to promote production, ensure sustainability, supply chain and increase capacity.
Mr. Pen Sovichet asserted that the implementation of this policy to be effective will be expected to help reduce costs and promote the competitiveness of Cambodian cashew products to a high level in the region and the world. This policy will be finalized soon.
