CMAC CEO: Research and development of Kovid 19 sniffing dogs is running smoothly, giving hope that Cambodia will have 19 Kovid 19 dogs soon
Mr. Heng Ratana confirmed this after he visited the dog center of CMAC unit in Kampong Chhnang to check the preparation activities to train the dogs to be infected with the virus 19, including the premises and dog training room.
He said that after receiving the initiative to research and develop 19 Kovid dogs from the Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the CMAC has set up a special training room for research. And develop 19 Kovid olfactory dogs, including 10 dog candidates, 10 dog owners, trainers and equipment to support the process.
He added that in the meantime, CMAC has documented the operational procedures, research, training and implementation of experimental studies, data analysis, capacity assessment and efficiency of dogs, which is an important soft element to determine the success of research and development. Development.
In addition, CMAC has partnered with the Ministry of Health and the University of Health Sciences to develop a number of procedures, and health scientists have made efforts to complete a number of technical protocols to remove odors from patients safely and professionally.
The CMAC unit also received a grant from the Swiss company to detect 19 Kovid virus-infected patients, and recently the health department began to apply Kovid olfactory devices to Kovid patients and continue to test for Kovid virus-free devices. This research and training does not affect the risk of infection.
Mr. Heng Ratana stated, "With the experience in using and training dogs, as well as the high commitment of health scientists and dog technicians, which has made this research and development moving forward quickly, on time and in the right direction. Towards our hope is to have 19 dogs fighting Kovid in Cambodia. ”
Please note that some countries such as Germany, Finland, Australia, Russia, etc. have been using dogs to help sniff out the carrier virus. Currently, CMAC has dogs capable of supporting counter-terrorism, mine and UXO clearance and environmental work.
