The provincial administration has called on the people of Stung Treng to take care of their health and follow the rules of the Ministry of Health. Australia, Germany and Switzerland will provide about $ 13 million to improve the overall condition of the Mekong Basin
(Phnom Penh): Australia, Germany and Switzerland have pledged about $ 13 million to the Mekong River Commission (MRC) to support Cambodia, Laos, Thailand and Vietnam in responding to urgent challenges while maintaining support. Ecological function of the Mekong River and improve people's livelihoods.
According to a press release issued by the Mekong River Commission on June 24, 2021, this contribution follows a new wave of development partner funds to the MRC as the commission begins implementing its five-year strategic plan. 2021-2025) under a new direction aimed at overcoming the many threats that the river basin is facing.
"This support is timely and very important for the MRC to implement activities that balance the needs of economic development, social improvement and environmental protection." This was stated by Dr. An Pich Hatta, Executive Director of the Secretariat at a signing ceremony during an informal development partnership meeting on Thursday in Vientiane.
Under the new strategic plan, the MRC will ensure that all new national plans for power generation weigh on a wide range of energy source options, including energy-food-water options, as well as additional use of solar and wind energy. . It will promote diversity, gender equality and the gender environment in the water sector.
The MRC will explore ways to facilitate the operation of water infrastructure for the entire river basin to maximize benefits and minimize negative environmental impacts on the Mekong River and its population.
It will also continue to promote regional dialogue among river basin countries and deepen engagement with China, as well as with other partners, through ASEAN-Lancang-Mekong cooperation for the benefit of All members.
"We hope that these grants will increase the MRC and its member countries' ability to use the water and related resources of the Mekong River in an environmentally and sustainable manner as part of its rehabilitation assistance," said Ambassador Kelly. "The economy recovers from the global epidemic, builds environmental resilience and strengthens regional cooperation."
He added that Australia is also providing technical support to the MRC in a number of key areas, including the Center for Flood and Drought Management and the improvement of its institutional database and information.
"We are confident that this fund will help the MRC establish a core network for river monitoring in the Mekong region and, as a result, contribute to information-based decision-making for water resources management and development," said Ambassador Lutken Hem. "Increase the MRC's ability to manage floods and droughts more effectively."
"We applaud the MRC for launching the new vision strategies needed for the Mekong Basin," he said. "We will work with the MRC, member states and other development partners to move the region towards a more sustainable and resilient path, ensuring that no one is left behind."
He added that Switzerland has supported the MRC since 1995 and believes it plays an important role in water cooperation in the Mekong region.
Please note that the MRC's five-year strategic plan is an essential tool for the Commission to implement a new 10-year Basin Development Strategy, which represents a turning point for the Mekong Basin. Together, it enables countries along the Mekong Basin to address emerging and emerging challenges while improving the overall condition of the Mekong Basin in the coming decades. Send your wishes to the Embassy of Japan
Tanabata, or Star Festival, is a traditional Japanese festival held on July 7 in Japan to commemorate the parable of a couple in heaven, a weaver and a cowboy. Separated by the Amanogawa River. They are only allowed to meet once a year, on July 7. On that day, Japanese people wrote their wishes and hung them on bamboo sticks to make those wishes come true.
During the "Online Star Festival 2021" in Cambodia, the Embassy of Japan will decorate bamboo trees at the Embassy of Japan. The Embassy of Japan invites the public to participate in the "Online Star Festival 2021" by sending various wishes from June 23 to July 1, 2021 to the Embassy.
All wishes sent will be hung on a bamboo branch at the Embassy of Japan with other decorations, and the bamboo will be featured in the video of the "Online Star Festival 2021".
