Cambodian diplomat: China's leading effort in multilateral vaccines attaches great importance
(Phnom Penh): Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation of Cambodia, said that China's leading efforts in multilateral vaccines are of great importance.
He was speaking at the Asia-Pacific Summit on Belt and Road Cooperation under the theme "Enhancing Cooperation in Fighting Epidemics for Sustainable Development" on June 23, 2021: At the invitation of State Councilor and Foreign Minister Wang Yi.
"China's leading efforts in multilateral vaccines are widely recognized and highly valued," he said. To date, China has distributed more than 350 million doses of the vaccine to global cooperation partners.
"By ensuring that anyone can get the vaccine, China will continue to support its vaccine makers in sharing technology and vaccine production with developing countries," he said. "With these strong commitments, we can see the future of a world safer than it is today."
On that occasion, Mr. Prak Sokhon also sincerely thanked the People's Republic of China for hosting the Asia-Pacific Summit on Belt and Road Cooperation on topics related to the world today.
He stressed that international cooperation and partnership building are more important than ever in our efforts to revitalize the economy and society and free it from disease-related socio-economic crises. The epidemic.
The Cambodian Foreign Minister firmly believes that international cooperation and connectivity are key catalysts for our recovery. Our regional cooperation needs to focus on ensuring that the regional supply chain remains strong. We do well in this context due to the many belt and road projects that are growing across the region.
He added that in Cambodia, we have a large special economic zone in Sihanoukville, the project to build the Phnom Penh-Sihanoukville Expressway and the new international airport in Phnom Penh and Siem Reap. These infrastructure projects have been progressing smoothly and will surely contribute to accelerating Cambodia's economic recovery.
Looking forward to the future of belt and road cooperation in the Asia-Pacific region, Mr. Prak Sokhon shared some ideas:
* First, we need to strengthen international cooperation and cooperation in the production and distribution of vaccines in a more environmentally friendly and equitable manner, and should avoid using the global pandemic as a political tool. Equally important, regional cooperation is needed to strengthen future pandemics.
* Second: Building a "Common Destiny Community for Humanity" is not just a political slogan, but an international co-operation that will help us achieve our sustainable development goals.
* Third: We need to maintain a multilateral trading and investment system based on the rule of law, as well as openness and environment. In this regard, the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement (RCEP) will play a key role in promoting "open and inclusive regionalism" and supporting a more inclusive and sustainable recovery.
* Fourth: We need to provide more resources for social protection. This social protection is not an expense or a prolongation of our prosperity, but rather an investment in our people that will help drive a more dynamic, environmentally friendly economy. I believe this is the true spirit of all belt and road projects.
While supporting the two initiatives proposed by China on vaccine cooperation and green development partnership, the Cambodian diplomat is optimistic that the spirit of belt and road cooperation will continue to contribute to the achievement. Our Sustainable Development Goals for many years to come.
