National Road Safety Committee and UNDP Disseminate New Report on Road Accidents in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): The National Road Safety Committee and the United Nations Development Program (UNDP) have released a new report on road accidents in Cambodia and the economic losses caused by these accidents, using data from 2019. From the victim and traffic accident information system.
Of all the vehicles involved in all registered accidents, more than 80 percent are motorcycles. Most of the deaths occurred on national roads and in the capital. When assessing the loss of life due to traffic accidents, the behavior of road users is a major factor.
"It does not matter whether the victim is a young man or a man, but it does involve the use of alcohol, drugs, disobedience to traffic laws and the use of mobile phones while driving," he said. "Overall, it depends on your behavior, which determines the loss of life in an accident."
She said that the cooperation to prevent and reduce traffic accidents is appropriate to the causes, location, time and economic loss of traffic accidents through related activities and missions, including the continued tightening of law enforcement, improving the technical conditions of roads, bridges, especially. Education, dissemination and so on.
This report shows that when we look at other factors, the largest variables associated with loss of life in an accident are caused by "four factors that lead to loss of life", including consumption. Alcohol and drugs, speeding, disobeying traffic rules and using the phone while driving. Human error contributes to 98% of accidents and 16% of accidents result in loss of life.
This report highlights the need for an immediate response. More than 80 percent of fatalities occur immediately at the scene. Only 35 percent of the victims received first aid, down 24 percent from 2018.
This report recommends response in the form of policies, such as accelerating policy formulation and strengthening data collection, improving infrastructure and legal documents, and strengthening traffic enforcement, particularly focusing on these factors. 4 leading to loss of life as described above, increasing awareness and change of driver behavior and implementation of measures to respond to emergencies.
