Residents say the commune chief's son's chicken farm smelled of flies, affecting the health and the noise of the chickens caused by the farm. Not only that, people living on the sidewalks are also affected by the environment by transporting chickens to the slaughterhouse at Chheang Tang's son's farm.
In connection with the villagers' claim, Mr. Chheang Tang, Prek Koy commune chief, said on the morning of June 22, 2021 that the location of his son's chicken farm was a solid plot of land that was not related to Stung Romia Chap as mentioned. The rise of the people.
On the other hand, environmental problems affect the lives of the people, he said that the farm was established more than 10 years ago, people have never complained about the stench, affecting them, while the people just complained. Not yet.
Chheang Tang admits that recently the stench emanating from the farm is real. The stench was caused by flooding of his son's chicken farm and the flooding of crops and people's homes and homes. He said that the water caused by the flood was caused by Peal's sand mine draining into the Romiep River.
However, residents still insist that the district authorities and relevant officials come down to investigate the encroachment on the river and ask for the law to be enforced on the chicken farm. As for Mr. Chheang Tang claiming that his son's farm has a solid title deed, the people asked him to release the title deed to the public because the people living along the river said that their land did not have a title deed. No.
