The approval came during the 60th Board Meeting of the Global Environment Facility (GEF) and the 30th GEF Board Meeting of the GEF.
These final projects will focus on supporting and addressing key environmental challenges such as soil erosion, biodiversity loss, unsustainable fisheries and climate change that have been threatening food security and livelihoods. Hundreds of thousands in Asia and Africa.
These projects will be implemented in partnership and co-financed by the governments of member countries such as Cambodia, Cameroon, Eritrea, Lesotho, Malaysia, Senegal, Thailand and Vietnam.
QU Dongyu, Director-General of the FAO, said: "These projects are especially welcomed after the launch of the United Nations Decade on Ecological Restoration Initiatives. It is now necessary to take steps to improve "Improving the natural systems on land and water that we rely on to achieve optimal nutrition, environment and life."
The GEF-approved project will support the eight countries and their communities in choosing sustainable and climate resilient practices, as well as promote regional cooperation and develop stronger policies to Conserve biodiversity and prevent illegal, unreported and unauthorized fishing (IUU).
These projects will directly benefit 441,500 people and restore more than 27,000 hectares of degraded landscapes. The projects will also create new protected areas on 30,000 hectares of land and sea and improve management of more than 765,000 hectares of landscaping and 4 million hectares of fish and marine habitats.
Activities planned in the project will contribute to reducing greenhouse gas emissions by 6.8 million tons and turning overfishing of fish stocks of 547,393 tons back to a sustainable level. .
Among the FAO-led projects are projects to be implemented in the Gulf of Thailand to promote sustainable fisheries management in Cambodia, Malaysia, Thailand and Vietnam.
By implementing an ecological approach that considers the fisheries sector and strengthens governance in the fisheries sector, these projects will help conserve marine biodiversity and reduce overfishing while supporting the sustainable livelihoods of fishermen.
Four projects approved for Africa will address the threat of climate change and soil erosion, increase community climate resilience, protect natural resources and strengthen the agricultural value chain to improve people's livelihoods. In the countryside.
