(Phnom Penh): Mr. Pov Polynel, Undersecretary of State of the Civil Aviation Secretariat in charge of the Department of Air Transport and the Department of Strategic and Policy Planning, shared his personal experience of receiving a PhD scholarship to study in Japan.
Despite being born into a poor family, Pov Polynel struggled to get a doctorate scholarship in Japan in 2008 and plans to graduate in 2021.
From his practical experience, there are four key points to get a doctoral scholarship to study in Japan, including:
4: Once you have completed the examination of the above documents and conditions, be prepared for the oral interview, focusing on defending our preliminary research project.
Mr. Pov Polinel described his brief educational background as "fortunately" as a child, his home was close to the school, enabling him to study, and the encouragement and encouragement from his family that knowledge was an asset. Good luck for those who like this study. He claims that his parents are willing to lend him money so that he can study, and he also strives to learn English.
After graduating from the 12th grade, he passed the Bachelor of History exam at the Royal University of Phnom Penh from 1998 to 2002. In 2001, he also applied for a legal scholarship at the Royal University of Law and Economics until 2005.
At the same time, after graduating with a bachelor's degree in history, Mr. Pov Polynel also received a scholarship from the Royal Academy of Cambodia for a master's degree in history from 2002 to 2004. He stated that he always teaches English in various schools to solve life before becoming a civil servant.
"As children of the poor, we do not have the means to earn a living or continue to study, so I gradually learned to work by teaching English in various schools to help support life during school."
In 2004, Mr. Pov Polynel became a civil servant at the Royal Academy after completing his master's degree. Later, from 2005 to 2006, he applied for another scholarship to become a +1 teacher at the National Institute of Education.
After passing the IELTS exam, he applied for a scholarship in Korea through the Korea Cooperation Agency (KOICA) in Cambodia to study for a Master's degree in Public Policy and Public Management from 2011 to 2013, before receiving a PhD in Japan from 2018 to 2021. He stressed that without the IELTS degree, the scholarship requirements could not be met.
