The agricultural sector remained relatively resilient due to strong demand accompanied by exports under the free trade agreement signed between Cambodia and China. The manufacturing sector has also gradually recovered, adapting to changing external conditions.
Wholesale and retail businesses also struggled slowly, as domestic economic activity gradually recovered. In contrast, service sectors such as accommodation, restaurants and transportation continue to be hit hard by the decline in domestic and international tourism.
Foreign direct investment continues to flow in and support the overseas sector. Going forward, Cambodia's outlook for growth remains highly uncertain, with the format and speed of the recovery process almost entirely dependent on the state of the virus and the speed of vaccination.
The report also looks at negative scenarios, including the possibility that vaccination programs are slow and that nationwide closure measures remain frequent and protracted. Under such conditions, the economy is projected to grow by only 1 percent in 2021.
The current report recommends that Cambodia take immediate action to enhance market credibility by accelerating vaccination programs while continuing efforts to effectively prevent viral infections and deaths.
Because Cambodia still has sufficient financial capacity, the government can continue to support relief, rehabilitation and growth programs by improving the business and investment environment, providing social protection programs, and promoting pro-poor public investment and economic growth.
In order to maintain the stability of the financial sector, it is important that government authorities continue to closely monitor the vulnerabilities that may result from the prolonged turmoil in the real estate sector and the increasing lending to the reparations sector.
