Dr. Kin Phea presents an important work that makes our Cambodian nation free from insults from other nations
(Phnom Penh): Looking back more than 1,000 years ago, the Cambodian people have always been proud of their nation, which was once a powerful empire in Southeast Asia, with vast territories, high civilization and great power, no nation can insult.
The preamble to the 1993 Cambodian Constitution also expresses this pride, inscribed: "The Khmer people once had a great civilization, a great nation, vast, prosperous, glorious, high, shining like diamonds."
The glorious Angkorian period is considered the golden age of Khmer history. The Khmer ancestors of that time built thousands of stone temples scattered throughout the country and also in neighboring countries today. The Cambodian people and their leaders have always been proud of these great legacies that have survived to the present day and have also benefited greatly from these great legacies.
Despite many political changes in Cambodia, Angkor Wat remains the source of the Khmer nation's highest pride and has been enshrined in the center of the Cambodian flag since 1947.
A thousand years later, pride is replaced by pain and no end in sight. That does not even count for the next 1,000 years. After the fall of Angkor, the Khmer Empire gradually weakened, and the territory gradually shrunk, almost disappearing from the world map. The nation has experienced both incurable warfare, internal warfare, and war with foreign invaders.
This made Cambodia, a powerful empire, fall into the status of a colony of neighboring states more than 1,000 years later. Many territories were lost to neighboring countries, in fact, the Khmer territory of dozens of provinces fell to Thailand, the territory of Kampuchea Krom, dozens of provinces, including Prey Nokor, fell to Vietnam and partly to the country. Laos, too.
After liberation from the colonies of neighboring states, Cambodia fell under French colonization for almost another century. The struggle to break free from the yoke of French colonization took decades to gain full independence in 1953. After gaining independence, Cambodia became an island of peace, but soon slipped into civil war for more than two decades. It was not until the end of 1998 that Cambodia was completely at peace.
When it comes to the past of the Khmer Empire and the beloved Angkorian civilization, all Cambodians are very proud of their history and ancestors. But when it comes to the tragedy of the past and the lost land, every Cambodian has no remorse and is in as much pain as a knife pierced through the heart.
But pride, pain, and regret are not enough. Instead of suffering and regret, the Cambodian people of all walks of life and all political tendencies must implement our commitment enshrined in our Constitution: "Awake, stand up, be strong, be united, be united "National unity protects the territory of Cambodia, precious sovereignty and the beloved civilization of Angkor."
Farmers must strive to grow crops, raise livestock, workers must be energetic in their work, students must strive to learn to be worthy of the national pillar, intellectuals must strive to study, research, publish, civil servants must strive to serve the people with a national conscience and code. Professional ethics.
In particular, professors and teachers must strive to teach students to be capable, productive, innovative, virtuous and have a national conscience, the army must be loyal to the nation, brave and resolute in the cause of defending national sovereignty and territorial integrity, and the police must be committed to maintaining security and good social order.
Politicians must think of the national interest and the people at large, think of the next generation, do not think only of the next election, the courts must maintain justice for the people, the monks must strive to educate social morality, and businessmen must have a code Moral and considerate of the environment and the planet, and other social components must strive to carry out their work with the highest national conscience and responsibility.
We are the descendants of our ancestors, we have inherited a great legacy from our ancestors, thousands of stone temples, for example, what legacy will we leave for this generation? That depends on all of our efforts now and then. We must create pride and leave a legacy for our descendants, just as our ancestors left us.
All of us Cambodians must be vigilant, not to dance in the wrong rhythm, not to jump in the wrong direction. Our country has suffered and suffered countless tragedies due to the ideological race of the superpowers and due to the erratic dancing and leaping of politicians in the past. We should not trample on history anymore. No country loves our nation more than we love our nation.
If our nation is weak, we cannot go and ask for peace from anyone. If our nation is weak, do not talk about independence and neutrality, even if we ask to live peacefully without disturbing any nation, it is not certain that our nation will be happy. Therefore, only by striving to make our nation strong can our nation escape the insults of other nations. We must work hard for the existence, progress and prosperity of our nation now and in the future.
For our nation to be strong, we must make our human resources strong, our laws strong, our political system strong, our economy strong, our defense strong, our national unity and pride strong, our diplomacy strong, our social justice system strong. And our peace is strong, then our nation will be strong, strong in the long run, not strong at one time.
(Phnom Penh): The Asian Development Bank (ADB) and the Cambodian Development Research and Training Institute (CDRI) have announced the first partnership to integrate ADB digital publications into CDRI's online library in conjunction with the program. Publish an important book on "Asia's Prosperity: More Than 50 Years of Marketing and Technology Policy".
In Cambodia, CDRI is the first knowledge center to be selected as a partner for the Official Deposit Library of the ADB Deposit Library Program. Cambodia is among the 15 countries across Asia and the Pacific selected for the program, which aims to promote knowledge and innovation to address national development issues.
Each quarter, ADB expects to share at least 50 publications with CDRI, including documents on economics and finance, transportation, trade, the environment and natural resources, education, gender, agriculture, and innovation.
During the launch of the Partnership for Online Deposit Library, ADB and CDRI also published a book on "The Road to Asia's Prosperity: More Than 50 Years of Marketing Policy and Technology." This book provides an overview of growth and change in Asia over the last 50 years and discusses key policy lessons drawn from regional experiences.
The ADB is currently implementing a $ 2.1 billion investment project in Cambodia through the Country Partnership Strategy 2019-2023, incorporating comprehensive technical assistance and knowledge programs. By 2021, ADB plans to publish 27 knowledge documents and organize 11 more knowledge dissemination programs, including documents and programs to support Cambodia's economic development and recovery from the 19 Kovid outbreak.
