Leaders of NATO member states made the joint commitment Monday as US President Joe Biden sought to restore Washington's transatlantic ties after a rift under the former presidential term. Donald Trump.
In a joint statement, NATO leaders said that China's growing activities in building nuclear weapons as well as space and information technology capabilities were threatening the international order.
In response to the statement, the Chinese envoy to the European Union called on NATO to rationalize China's development, stop exaggerating the theory that China is a threat to the world and not take advantage of its legitimate interests. China acts as an excuse to take political advantage for its group and at the same time create confrontation.
Following last weekend's meeting in the United Kingdom, the G7 leaders, most of them NATO members, also issued joint statements attacking China over Xinjiang, Hong Kong and Taiwan. China in London has come out in condemnation of the G7 statement. General Knowledge: What motivated the United States to achieve its ambition to send people to the moon in the 1960s?
When Kennedy announced in 1961 his commitment to send people to the moon, the United States and the Soviet Union were in the throes of a Cold War, and space was the place to be. Importantly, sending people to the moon will be a huge success for both countries.
At the time, US media reported that the launch of these satellites would provide a military advantage for the Soviets in the Cold War between the two sides. We can say that the space race between the two world powers has also begun.
On May 25, 1961, Kennedy proposed and officially announced his ambition to send humans to the moon and bring them back to Earth safely before the new decade. Later, the Apollo space program took shape, and the US president predicted that its success would prompt the whole world to choose the United States as a model country rather than the Soviet Union.
Although the Apollo program was created in the spirit of competition with the Soviet Union, there have been several attempts to ease the space race for diplomatic reasons. In September 1963, Kennedy said the United States and the Soviet Union could still work together on the Apollo program. But the possibility of establishing space cooperation between the two sides disappeared after Kennedy was assassinated two months later.
The United States finally achieved the greatest achievement that the Soviet Union could not achieve, and it was the turning point in the history of American space leadership in the world from that time to the present.
