Australia decides to increase investment in Cambodia's water sector to supply rural people
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Luke Arnold, Chargé d'Affaires of the Australian Embassy in Cambodia, this morning, June 15, 2021, met with HE Dr. Cham Prasidh, Senior Minister, Minister of Industry, Science, Technology and Innovation to promote bilateral cooperation. Access to clean water for people in the provinces in Cambodia.
Mr. Arnold added that Australia will continue to work closely with the Royal Government of Cambodia to further develop the water sector because of the direct impact on people's health and livelihoods when they have access to clean water at home. . This important study highlights important opportunities to expand water supply through collaboration and partnerships between public and private institutions.
Mr. Arnold added that the Australian Embassy's contribution to Cambodia's water supply network is consistent with Australia's infrastructure development program in Cambodia, including the prioritization of a number of projects that are funded sustainably and that provide real benefits. Sure for the people of Cambodia.
Dr. Cham Prasidh also stated that the government plans to provide 100% clean water to the people in the town by 2025 and provide clean water to all Cambodians by 2030. With the database that 3i has developed with the ministry's director general of water supply, the ministry is on the right track to achieve this goal.
He also thanked Australia for all the assistance and work provided to the Ministry.
The study was conducted by the Australian Infrastructure Investment Program (3i), which has a total of A $ 49 million in Cambodia. The study assessed the scope of water supply across Cambodia's 22 provinces and calculated the total investment to supply clean water to all villages in Cambodia that do not yet have access to clean water.
It should be noted that through Program (3i), Australia has approved and invested more than A $ 19.8 million to nearly 80 private water operators to establish and expand water supply to rural households. By the end of the program, more than one million Cambodians will have access to clean water thanks to the 3i program.
The Ministry of Tourism announces the selection of candidates to compete in the "Young Entrepreneurs in Rural Tourism" program of the World Tourism Organization.
The competition aims to recruit outstanding young entrepreneurs from WTO member countries to promote the establishment of tourism businesses, especially in rural tourism destinations, to support the livelihoods of local people towards the development of sustainable tourism. And better resilience, especially to address the impact of the Kovid-19 crisis on tourism.
Winners will receive technical assistance to strengthen their business from the World Tourism Organization, become members of the World Young Entrepreneurs Network and have the opportunity to present their business to world tourism leaders from around the world. From 159 countries and major world organizations on the occasion of the 24th World Tourism Organization General Assembly in October 2021 in Morocco.
Candidates must present the features of their business, highlighting the level of innovation, addressing the impact on the lives of the affected people, Kovid-19, as well as contributing to sustainable development at the local level.
The Ministry of Tourism encourages young people who are young entrepreneurs who are doing business in tourism or planning to create businesses that help rural communities recover from the Kovid-19 crisis to participate in the above program and the Ministry of Tourism will cooperate in coordinating in Contest submissions.
The following is the announcement of the Ministry of Tourism: KOICA provides food and vaccination equipment for Kovid 19 for nearly $ 40,000 to vulnerable people in Cambodia
(Korea): The Korea International Cooperation Agency (KOICA), the agency representing South Korea's development cooperation, has provided food packages and 19 kovid infection prevention equipment worth a total of $ 38,000. To vulnerable people in Cambodia who face difficulties in their daily lives and studies due to the recurrence of Kovid 19.
According to a KOICA press release, the assistance was provided to 627 vulnerable families, orphans (only parents) and about 3,400 poor families. The aid provided includes disinfection and disinfection equipment, a personal hygiene card, 25 kg of rice, fish and other essentials made locally.
KOICA Cambodia Office provides food packages of locally produced food to partner institutions in nine capitals and provinces that have volunteered to distribute to vulnerable people (poor card students, orphans, People with disabilities and families with many members, etc.). In addition, KOICA has provided temperature and alcohol disinfection devices to the institution to create a safe environment within the institution.
In this assistance project, in collaboration with the partner institutions, the selection of priority persons based on the conditions of the poor families is very difficult, and the actual distribution is carried out in accordance with the hygiene measures of the partner institutions.
Not only does KOICA Cambodia provide this emergency assistance to prevent students from dropping out of school and to enhance their personal awareness of communicable diseases in the institution, we also encourage volunteer activities to be taught through E-volunteering, which started in July 2020 and continues to this day.
