(Preah Vihear): Update on the results of the nationwide vulture census on June 10, 2021, experts recorded the presence of 82 vultures, including 13 vultures, 42 gray vultures and 27 brown vultures. This was stated by Mr. Net Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesman of the Ministry of Environment on June 12, 2021, after the opening of the vulture census on June 10.
According to Mr. Net Pheaktra, the presence of vultures recorded at the four census sites includes: First, 23 vultures were recorded at the vulture restaurant in Preah Vihear province, and second: no vultures were present at the canteen. Mondulkiri Province: 3: 52 vultures were recorded at the vulture restaurant in Stung Treng province and 4: 7 vultures were recorded at the vulture restaurant in Kratie province.
"To conclude that the population of the world's endangered vulture population is declining or increasing, the technical working group of the Ministry of Environment and partner organizations will continue to conduct a nationwide census," the spokesman said. The next five are scheduled for June 20, September 10, September 20, December 10, December 20 and December 20, 2021.
A senior official of the Ministry of Environment pointed out that in Preah Vihear province, the Ministry of Environment, in collaboration with WCS, has carried out conservation activities in protected areas by providing extra food twice a month to vultures and protecting their nests. In-depth understanding of vulture risk factors in Preah Vihear province.
From January to May 2021, there will be 10 supplementary feedings through "vulture canteens" to monitor the number of vultures. There are only three species of vultures in Cambodia: first: fire vultures, second: brown vultures and three: gray vultures. Vultures, the most endangered species from Earth, are present in Preah Vihear, Stung Treng, Mondulkiri and Kratie provinces of Cambodia.
Meanwhile, Mr. Net Pheaktra said that the reasons why vultures in Cambodia are declining due to the greatest threat from human pollution, loss of habitat, lack of food, hunting through traps. And pollution.
Mr. Pheaktra stated that communities in some areas have used poisons in or around ponds to catch birds and mammals, and that pollution is also a major cause of vulture deaths. They ate the corpses of dead animals due to poison.
The Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment said that on behalf of the Ministry of Environment, he called on all people to participate in the conservation and protection of vultures by stopping hunting or wildlife trade.
The spokesperson stated that vultures are gentle and clean-up by eating other animal carcasses as food and protecting and conserving vultures, which is a great preservation of Cambodia's precious natural heritage. And the whole world as a value for biodiversity conservation and attracting tourists for eco-tourism, creating jobs, providing income to the people.
