International Analysis: US Child Labor Problems Darken 'Human Rights Beacon'
As International Labor Day and the United Nations Children's Fund (UNICEF) release the latest report showing that by 2021, the number of child laborers worldwide will be 20 June. The world has grown for the first time in 20 years.
At the same time, during the 109th International Labor Video Conference in Geneva, US issues such as forced labor and child labor were widely criticized. Many analysts have noted that in matters where global child labor is exacerbated, the United States has an undeniable responsibility.
Child abuse is only a small part of US systemic human rights abuses. According to the data, more than 100,000 people are trafficked from abroad to the United States each year. In the last five years, all 50 states and territories of the United States have reported cases of forced labor and human trafficking.
Of the eight major conventions of the International Labor Organization (ILO), only two have been ratified by the United States, with the fewest member states ratifying it. Moreover, the United States is also the only country in the world that does not authorize the "United Nations Convention on the Rights of the Child".
It can be said that either labor rights or child protection have become instruments of sacrifice in "exceptions of the United States." The United States claims to be the "beacon of human rights" in the world, but has perpetuated systematic labor rights abuses. 18th century.
Some US politicians often talk about "rules" orally and also fabricate lies, slander other countries as "forced labor" and "human rights abuses". But in fact, the country that despises international law and violates human rights is the United States. Cambodian Red Cross in Svay Rieng Province Receives Funds from Donors to Celebrate the 158th Anniversary of World Red Cross Red Crescent Day, May 8
