The Ministry of Health of Cambodia would like to inform the public and authorities at all levels about the situation of chicken fever in Cambodia as of June 9, 2021 as follows:
1: Chest fever status
According to the National Dengue Monitoring System, as of June 9, 2021, 514 cases of suspected dengue fever had been reported through 13 dengue surveillance systems, of which 86% of these cases came from Ratanakkiri, Mondulkiri, Battambang and Kratie provinces.
The results of the biological study showed that in and around the house, the presence of water worms formed by mosquitoes, which are carriers of chickenpox and dengue fever, accounted for 32% in round jars and cans, coconut shells, rice bowls, plastic cups, and plastic cups. Tires and motorcycle tires left in the water.
2: Measures to prevent chicken fever
In order to prevent the spread of leprosy caused by leopard mosquitoes more effectively, Prof. Mam Bunheng, Minister of Health, reminded, encouraged and recommended to all citizens and local authorities to increase participation. Move to reduce and prevent mosquito breeding by implementing the following measures:
Measure 1: Parents, guardians of all children, including local authorities, must actively participate in getting rid of water worms in places where water is left unused, such as car tires, motorcycles, bottle caps, coconut shells, rice bowls, soup cans, coffee cans, buried or burned. Dispose of or store in a way to prevent waterlogging.
Step # 2- Discovering Your Purpose There is no such thing as a "clean" home. Can easily bite us. During the day it is necessary to wear long-sleeved clothes, sleep under a mosquito net regularly day and night and not to sit near dark places.
Measure 3: In case of fever above 38 degrees Celsius, take the patient to the state examination and treatment (public service) such as the nearest health center and hospital, do not try to treat patients with high fever. Immediately by yourself or postpone medical follow-up in a private service that does not have the ability to contract this fever.
Measure 4: All 25 Capital-Provincial Health Departments must manage the provision of services and facilities for the treatment of malaria in the hospital well and refer the patients to other hospitals in case there is a large number of malaria patients. Overcrowding in any hospital, leaving no delay in accessing examination and treatment services.
