On June 10, 2021, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Head of the Royal Government of Cambodia, sent a message to his compatriots on the good news of the first success of the gold mining business, which can produce about 3 tons of pure gold per year. .
Samdech Techo Prime Minister has decided to announce the operation of gold mining business and gold refinery in O'Khvav area, Keo Seima district, Mondulkiri province from June 21.
Samdech Techo, the head of the Royal Government, added that the study showed that the gold ore that the company has already studied can produce about 3 tons of pure gold per year for the first eight years. The company expects to be able to expand further after this initial period.
Samdech Techo also said that the gold mines that the company operates will provide an average gross income of about $ 185 million per year, of which the national budget revenue from royalties and other taxes averages about $ 40 million. Per year.
Samdech Techo emphasized that these funds will be used in the national budget for economic and social development.
Senior environmental official: Vulture numbers continue to decline every year, worrying
(Preah Vihear): Vultures are a rare species and are at high risk of extinction globally as the number of vultures continues to decline every year and to a worrying extent, despite the Royal Government through the Ministry The environment, in collaboration with partner organizations, strives to protect and conserve. This was confirmed by Mr. Net Pheaktra, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment during the 2021 Vulture Census on June 10.
There are only three species of vultures in Cambodia: first: fire vultures, second: brown vultures and third, gray vultures. Vultures are the most endangered species on earth, but are present in Preah Vihear, Stung Treng, Mondulkiri, Ratanakkiri and Kratie provinces of Cambodia.
Mr. Net Pheaktra said that in 2020, the Ministry of Environment cooperated with international organizations to conduct a vulture census five times a year. According to the National Vulture Census in 2020, the largest number of vultures was only 129 in Cambodia, and they live in herds and eat carcasses.
"The reasons for the decline in vultures in Cambodia are due to the greatest threat from human pollution, habitat loss, food shortages, poaching and pollution," said Neth Pheaktra.
A senior official of the Ministry of Environment confirmed that "rangers found 61 vultures on May 31, 2021, including 40 gray vultures, 2 fire vultures and a number of brown vultures." 19 at the vulture restaurant in Dang Phlit village in Chep Wildlife Sanctuary during the monthly feeding.
He added: "Preliminary results of the vulture census in the Chep Wildlife Sanctuary on June 9, experts are summarizing the results after the census and release the report in time. Coming soon. ”
At the same time, the spokesman of the Ministry of Environment said that the census is a survey of biodiversity in protected areas in Cambodia to find out what biodiversity wildlife is present in the protected area to facilitate. Effective protection and conservation planning aims to preserve the biodiversity that Cambodia has.
According to Mr. Net Pheaktra, in Preah Vihear province, the Ministry of Environment, in collaboration with WCS, has carried out conservation activities in protected areas by providing extra food twice a month to vultures and protecting their nests. In-depth understanding of risk factors for vultures in Preah Vihear province.
He said that from January to May 2021, there will be 10 supplementary feed arrangements through "vulture restaurants" to monitor the number of vultures. Vultures are the most endangered species in the world, but are still present in Cambodia.
Three Higher Education Institutions Collaborate to Organize Online Workshops in EU Eleanor Project
(Battambang): Three Cambodian higher education institutions, including the National University of Battambang, National University of Meanchey and the Institute of Technology of Cambodia, have co-organized an online training workshop on active and problem-based learning methods in engineering education in Cambodia. On June 10, 2021.
The training workshop is part of the European Union Higher Education Capacity Building Project (Erasmus + ALIEN). There were 60 participants who were teachers and researchers from National University of Battambang, National University of Meanchey, Institute of Technology of Cambodia and Battambang Institute of Pedagogy.
Ms. Sieu Srey Mean is currently a teacher at the Institute of Technology of Cambodia as a trainer in this training workshop. Dr. Sam Rany, Mr. Pich Veasna and Mr. Lay Heng were the workshop coordinators and project coordinators for the three higher education institutions.
In addition, the project aims to help develop students' professional skills and life skills according to their aspirations. The project also aims to encourage higher education institutions to develop their own strategic plans or educational policies by incorporating active and problem-based learning approaches in their engineering education programs.
The project lasts for more than three years (2017 to 2021), with implementation in five European countries and five Asian countries. The project has a total of 17 participating universities and 10 countries, including Greece, Portugal, the United Kingdom, Estonia, Bulgaria, Cambodia, Malaysia, Vietnam, Nepal and Pakistan. Prey Veng Provincial Administration Finds 42 More Kovid 19 Positive Cases
