The decline of US military power in the region in the face of increasing Chinese influence is one of the reasons pushing Japan on the path to becoming one of the leading military powers in the Asia-Pacific region. But Japan's militarization has also raised concerns in Russia, which has not signed a peace treaty with Japan since the end of World War II and is embroiled in a long-running territorial dispute. Around the Kuril Islands.
For decades, Japan has been considered a pacifist country that relies entirely on the United States for national security. Such a position is clearly stated in two important documents. The first is Article 9 of the 1947 Japanese Constitution, which states that "the Japanese people must always oppose war and that military force can only be used for the purpose of reconciling international conflicts."
However, analysts see that Japan's militarization is not a threat to Russia, for two reasons: The current Japanese military budget is not alarmingly large. That is, Japan's military budget has not increased by more than 1 percent of GDP, compared with the United States' military budget of 3.7 percent of its GDP by 2020 and Russia's 4.3 percent.
On the other hand, the development of Japanese military capabilities is unlikely to be aimed at Russia. In recent years, Tokyo has been very tolerant of Russian military action, including the deployment of bombers, sometimes flying into Japanese airspace. According to the Japanese military (Japanese fighter jets intercepted 206 Russian fighter jets from April to December 2020).
But Japan has not used the Russian fighter jets as an excuse to increase military activity along the border with Russia. The use of military force in addition to self-defense is unacceptable among Japanese leaders and the public. Japan has deployed new Marines in the town of Sasebo on the southwestern island of Kyushu, 1,700 km from the disputed archipelago with Russia.
