(Tbong Khmum): On the occasion of National and World Environment Day, June 5, 2021, the Ministry of Environment and Suong Municipality of Tbong Khmum Province cooperated to plant luxury trees and flowers to increase greenery for the city and cultivate the spirit. People love and participate in protecting the environment and conserving natural resources and biodiversity.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that the activity of planting trees and rare flowers today is a good activity that is valued as raising awareness of the environment and natural resources and planting trees together. Add in the community and participate in the protection and conservation of natural resources, forests, wildlife, biodiversity of all kinds.
"Joint tree planting in communities and towns not only creates greener shade and mitigates the effects of climate change, but also contributes to the care and conservation of important rosewood species in Cambodia," said the Secretary of State. In the community and contribute to increase the presence of rosewood in Cambodia and leave a legacy for future generations.
A spokesman for the Ministry of Environment said that every year, hundreds of thousands of rosewood trees, including rosewood, beng, neang, nuon, thnong, koki, and teal, are planted by local authorities in various parts of Cambodia, both on public roads and in schools. Pagodas and in degraded forest areas.
The senior official called the vigorous tree-planting activities across the country a movement to love the environment and natural resources, and to actively participate in reforestation as a gesture of respect, love and protection. Preserve natural resources and biodiversity for the benefit of future generations.
At the same time, Mr. Net Pheaktra said, "The trees that we all planted today means that Cambodia will have new trees in addition to the existing forest resources in the protected areas that we both "Everyone must participate in protecting and conserving."
The Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment called on people across the country: "Let all Cambodians join in planting trees to create greenery, increase forest cover and protect Cambodia's natural forest resources."
According to Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Cambodia has been striving to protect its natural resources by establishing several protected areas and conservation areas. To date, more than 70 protected areas and biodiversity corridors have been established. More than 7.3 million hectares, or more than 41 percent of Cambodia's total land area, are under the jurisdiction of the Ministry of Environment.
Suong City of Tbong Khmum Province is one of the districts in Cambodia that is active in creating green cover and participating in the conservation of natural resources, according to Mr. Chea Naron, Governor of Suong City. Neang Nuon rosewood, along with more than 3,000 yellow peacock and antelope trees, have been planted on the streets of Suong. The municipal administration will continue to plant more trees next year.
At the same time, the Suong Mayor also thanked Mr. Say Sam Al, Minister of Environment, for supporting the initiative of the municipal administration and providing luxury saplings, including yellow peacock and antelope trees to be planted in the city. .
