LONDON (Reuters) - A new study in the United Kingdom has found that the Arctic ice sheet of the Arctic is getting thinner and thinner faster than previously thought. This was released by the AFP news agency on Friday evening, June 4, 2021.
An analysis led by researchers at the University of UCL in the United Kingdom has concluded that the Arctic ice sheet is thinning at a rate of 70-100% faster than scientists have ever collected. Opinions conclude in the past. The team used a map with the latest data on the depth of snow on the glacier.
The thickness of the glaciers can be estimated by measuring their height on the surface of the water, but in order for this measurement to be accurate, the depth of the snow on them must also be weighed. Scientists have long used maps of snow depths in the Arctic Ocean, excluding the effects of climate change, say UCL researchers.
The Arctic Ocean is home to millions of square kilometers of glaciers, which are critical to lowering our planet's temperature, but that temperatures are currently rising. Declining Arctic ice sheets are also fueling geopolitical tensions as countries around the region look to the Arctic and its potential as a new transit route. The sea.
