Phnom Penh authorities are nearing completion of the excavation of the nearly 17-kilometer-long O'Ksach canal, while the Boeung Har canal is 100 percent restored to relieve flooding this rainy season
(Phnom Penh): The Phnom Penh Municipal Authority has been digging and rehabilitating the O Khsach canal, which is about 17 kilometers long, from Bek Chan commune, Ang Snoul district, Kandal province, to Sangkat Kambol, Sangkat Ovulok, Sangkat Snor, Khan Kambol, and continues to flow into Ta Mok Lake. Relieve water from flooding on National Road 4 and in front of Phnom Penh Airport.
Rehabilitation of Boeung Ha Canal, which is more than 2 km long, from the stone bridge on Prey Sar Road in Sangkat Dangkor, Khan Dangkor, Phnom Penh to the five drainage pumping stations. This rainy season.
Mr. Mao Bunthoeun, Director of the Department of Water Resources and Meteorology of Phnom Penh, said that the project to restore the canal is 16,799 meters long, 26 meters high, 17 meters deep and 3 meters deep in Ang Snoul district, Kandal province, 2,049 meters long. And Khan Kambol, Phnom Penh, 14,750 meters long, from Bek Chan commune, Ang Snoul district, Kandal province, then Sangkat Kambol, Sangkat Ovulok, Sangkat Snor, Khan Kambol and continues to flow into Ta Mok Lake.
Mr. Mao Bunthoeun confirmed that O'Ksach canal really plays an important role in releasing water along National Road 4, that is, water that falls from Kampong Speu province along National Road 4 to Bek Chan commune, Ang Snoul district. Turn the waterway to Ta Mok Lake, which reduces the amount of water flowing into Phnom Penh, which used to cause flooding in front of Pochentong Airport in the past.
Mr. Mao Bunthoeun continued that under the guidance of Mr. Khuong Sreng, Governor of Phnom Penh, the construction of O Khsach canal was started by the Phnom Penh Department of Water Resources in July 2020 and as of June 4, 2021, the working group of The Phnom Penh Department of Water Resources and Meteorology has excavated more than 15 kilometers, with only 2 kilometers left to complete the project.
In particular, the Boeung Ha canal, which is 2,580 meters long, 25 meters to 30 meters wide, from the stone bridge on Prey Sar Road to the drainage pump station in Sangkat Dangkor, Khan Dangkor, Phnom Penh, and this Boeung Ha canal really plays an important role in Release water from people's villages by releasing rainwater in parts of Khan Dangkor, Khan Por Senchey and Khan Meanchey.
In fact, the Phnom Penh Municipal Authority has now restored the Boeung Ha canal 100 percent to relieve the flooding during the rainy season. Boeung Ha Canal was rehabilitated by the team of Department of Water Resources and Meteorology of Phnom Penh on March 27, 2021 for more than 2 months and as of June 3, 2021, the whole site is 100% completed. Also.
