The Ministry of Health confirmed that the series of deaths in Pursat province's Krakor district was due to them consuming white wine or drugs containing high levels of methanol, according to laboratory results.
At the same time, the emergency response team and relevant authorities have educated and instructed both wholesale and retail sellers to suspend the business of white wine and drugs without proper supervision by the competent authorities and educated and advised the people in In the villages and communes where the incident took place, be extremely careful and immediately stop drinking alcohol that is not properly produced or sealed in accordance with technical regulations.
Due to the recurrence of methanol-related deaths in the first quarter of 2021, there were 14 deaths in Lvea Em district, Kandal province from 8 to 23 months. In May, 14 people died, 10 people died in Kampong Trach district, Kampot province, and 7 people died in Krakor district, Pursat province, a total of 31 people died due to alcohol poisoning.
Therefore, Prof. Mam Bunheng, Minister of Health, would like to remind and remind and urge all citizens and stakeholders throughout the capital-province to implement the following measures to prevent alcohol poisoning that is not properly closed. Technically and save its life:
* Do not drink wine that is closed or mixed without proper technique and without control, especially drinking wine or alcohol mixed with methanol.
For business people, it is strictly forbidden to mix methanol to make white wine, which causes poisoning and death.
As for the provincial, district, municipal, Khan, commune / sangkat authorities, they must assign a task force to control, monitor and prevent the sale of liquor or mixes without proper techniques and instruct the people to avoid using methanol mixed with white wine, which causes poisoning and death. Life.
Please be informed that this case of alcohol poisoning occurred in San Sor village and Thkol Thom village, Ansa Chambok commune, Krakor district, Pursat province, resulting in 13 deaths and many more illnesses by June 3, 2021.
