The Department of Livestock and Fisheries of the Lao Ministry of Agriculture and Forestry issued a notice on Monday announcing the ban, which will remain in force until the end of June. The ban was imposed after the department found cases of skin tumors on a cattle ranch in Vientiane, Laos, late last month, according to a report in the Vientiane Times on Thursday.
It should be noted that skin tumors are an infectious disease that affects cattle. It is transmitted by vampires such as flies, mosquitoes and lice. It causes fever, lumps on the skin and can even cause them to die. In a statement, the Lao department issued instructions on setting up checkpoints to control cattle transport until the epidemic situation is reduced.
The department also informed the general public, farmers and businesses about a number of ways to prevent the spread of the disease, including pest control, vampires and improving environmental hygiene on farms.
