(Phnom Penh): The Phnom Penh Capital Administration has announced that under the grant aid of the Government of Japan, it has started to implement the project to develop the sewage system in Phnom Penh, which is the first project to treat wastewater into Choeung Ek Lake. Properly aimed at contributing to the improvement of water quality, sanitation and living environment of the southern part of Phnom Penh and the area around Choeung Ek Lake.
According to the announcement of the Phnom Penh Capital Administration on June 2, the development process of the drainage system is scheduled to be completed by mid-November 2023.
The announcement states that the development project will be implemented on four main projects, including:
First, fill about 3 hectares of land next to Neak Oknha Moha Pheakdey Boulevard, Hun Neang Blvd. in Chak Angre Leu Sangkat, Meanchey District to build Choeung Ek Wastewater Treatment Plant.
Second: Construction of Choeung Ek wastewater treatment plant with a combined capacity of 5,000 cubic meters of wastewater per day.
Third: Construction and equipment of wastewater pump motor next to the drainage pump from Boeung Trabek pumping station.
Fourth, pouring soil to build a road for the maintenance of a 1.7-kilometer-long, 10-meter-wide canal and a 7-meter-wide asphalt pavement to dig a 0.30-meter-long steel pipe with a length of 1.84 kilometers from the sewage pumping plant to the treatment station.
Phnom Penh Capital Administration said that in order to start the implementation of the project, the construction company will prepare to build an office for the construction of a warehouse and a temporary storage area for construction materials on Neak Oknha Moha Pheakdey Boulevard, Hun Neang Boulevard and on the road for canal maintenance. .
The Phnom Penh Capital Administration hopes and believes in the spirit of understanding and good cooperation of the people and all institutions in Phnom Penh for the successful implementation of the above project.
The implementation of this project is expected to help better release water in Phnom Penh and avoid flooding during heavy rains during the rainy season.
