(Phnom Penh): The Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, has asked the United States to review the agreement regarding the repatriation of Cambodians living in the United States when they have committed a crime.
The Royal Government of Cambodia and the United States Government signed an agreement to repatriate Cambodians from the United States in 2002. Under this agreement, the US government will repatriate any Cambodian who commits an offense after serving his sentence.
But at the end of 2017, the repatriation of Cambodian citizens from the United States failed because the United States did not comply with Cambodia's request to amend some of the terms of the agreement on a voluntary basis.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen has protested a lot because he considered this condition to be the deprivation of Cambodian families living in the United States.
Eang Sophalleth, Samdech Techo Hun Sen's personal assistant, confirmed that in a meeting with the Deputy Foreign Minister this afternoon, Samdech Techo Hun Sen asked the administration of President Jo Biden to reconsider this condition.
According to Mr. Eang Sophalleth, the US Deputy Secretary of State agreed to take Samdech Techo Hun Sen's request to Mr. Biden for consideration and consideration.
