(Phnom Penh): The 19-month Kovid vaccination campaign in six districts in Phnom Penh, a former red zone, was given to more than 900,000 workers as the first dose was scheduled to close this week. And.
Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen has ordered the army to launch a vaccination campaign for the general population in the former Red Zone in Phnom Penh on May 1, 2021. Vaccination in the former Red Zone, an area at risk of contracting Kovid 19, so that people can be vaccinated to protect their health from the Kovid 19 pandemic.
The former Red Zone is located in six districts: Kampoul, Dangkor, Por Senchey, Meanchey, Toul Kork and Russey Keo. The former Red Zone arrow, which was initially vaccinated in four districts, had a population of just over 500,000, but was later expanded to six districts, with more than 1 million people vaccinated. .
Vaccination in the former Red Zone is scheduled to last only one month for both the first and second doses. But this time has been extended to June as the venue has been expanded and the number of players has been increased.
Although the vaccination operation in the former Red Zone has not been completely shut down, General Ith Sarath, Deputy Commander-in-Chief of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces and Chief of the Vaccination Task Force of the Ministry of National Defense, considers the work This is a great achievement and a milestone for the army.
The Red Zone is a complex area and faces a high prevalence of 19 Kovid cases, which is why the Royal Government has provided vaccination operations in the former Red Zone to the military. More than 3,000 troops from various units of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces (RCAF) have been deployed to vaccinate the people and implement the Royal Government's vaccination plan.
Brigadier General Tek Chansery, Deputy Director of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces Institute of Health Sciences Institute, was vaccinated by a 26-member unit in Trapeang Thleung village, Choam Chao 1 commune, Por Sen Chey district. Brigadier General Chan Serey and his comrades have been working at the former Red Zone vaccination site in Trapeang Thleung village for a month.
He said that vaccination in this area faces some difficulties, such as people do not understand the vaccination procedure, rush to get vaccinated only on weekends, which leads to congestion and distribution of vaccination cards to people. There is a delay and so on. These issues require your team to work harder to explain and complete the task.
Vaccination in the red zone, which is at risk for Kovid 19 infection, is also not an easy process. Vaccination teams require extreme caution by wearing tight-fitting clothing, masks, and masks.
Although vaccination in the red zone is complicated, tiring, no holidays, no Saturdays, Sundays, Brigadier General Tek Chansiri and his comrades still enjoy the vaccination mission in the red zone. The army sees the vaccination mission in the Red Zone as an important mission, and what they want from this operation is to save people from the risk of contracting Kovid 19.
The vaccine is considered by the Royal Government to be a strategic weapon in the fight against Kovid 19 and to protect the lives of the people from the Kovid 19 pandemic. Residents in the former Red Zone, who have already received two doses of the vaccine, have shown confidence in the vaccine to protect their health.
Not only do they rely on vaccines to protect their health, but they also hope that the vaccine will help prevent the spread of Kovid 19 and return to normal socio-economic processes so that they can earn a living. Better recovery. Union of Youth Federations of Cambodia issues statement celebrating International Children's Day this year
In its statement, the CPPCC considers children to be an essential human resource for the nation's future, so caring for children is a matter of thinking and planning for the future. Nation.
The CJCC reminded that during the war, especially in Democratic Kampuchea, the fundamental rights of the child were not fully protected. Children have no access to education and are forced to work hard, suffer severe torture, starvation, and especially be brutally slaughtered. Even after the end of the regime, the scars of the regime still have serious consequences for children.
Many children are orphaned and malnourished, which continues to be a consequence of their physical and mental development. After achieving complete peace, the rights of the child have been promoted and given high attention until now in accordance with the Cambodian Constitution, Rectangular Strategy, the Royal Government's political program and other plans.
In particular, in the phase of the outbreak of Kovid 19, which has a negative impact on the health, living conditions and education of children, families, the Union of Youth Federations of Cambodia throughout the country have contributed as much as possible with the Royal Government and all relevant authorities. Hierarchy in implementing necessary measures to reduce the impact on the general population, including the impact on children and families. According to the statement.
