With this speech, Mr. Park Heng Kheng said that President Moon Jae-in, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister Hun Sen and about 60 heads of state and leaders of international organizations will also attend the summit. .
"Cambodia is rich in natural resources," he said. This is the key to food security and people's livelihood. But it is threatened by climate change. "Climate change, in particular, poses a continuing threat to sustainable development in countries that rely heavily on the Mekong and the Tonle Sap."
He said the planet and its people are suffering from severe climate change. In fact, climate change is primarily caused by human activities. According to the World Meteorological Organization's report on climate change in 2020, global average temperatures will rise by 1.2 degrees Celsius compared to pre-industrial levels (basically from 1850 to 1900).
In response to global efforts to tackle climate change, the Republic of Korea has announced its ambitious plan to achieve its goal of carbon neutrality by 2050.
He added that in this context, the Republic of Korea will host the second P4G Summit online on May 30 and 31 this year under the theme "Green Recovery". Towards carbon neutrality. ”
P4G, which represents a partner for green growth and the 2030 Global Goals, aims to accelerate solutions to the five major P4G sectors (food, water, energy, cities and the circular economy). These are closely linked to the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals and are key elements in the global response to climate change.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra continued that this is a joint effort on green restoration and conservation of natural resources as part of supporting sustainable socio-economic development, contributing to reducing the impact of climate change that Cambodia and The world has been suffering.
The spokesman for the Ministry of Environment said that Cambodia is among the countries in Asia that are most vulnerable to climate change, while institutional capacity to mitigate and adapt to climate change is still limited. This is a risk that could jeopardize the pace of economic growth and sustainable development in the long run if we do not step up joint efforts.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra stated that in this regard, the Royal Government of Cambodia considers the response to climate change as an important priority, including in the Rectangular Strategy Phase 4 and the National Strategic Development Plan (2019-2023).
Two new research papers, “Resolving Climate Change Impacts on Cambodia's Economic Growth” and “Reducing Investment Risks in Cambodia's Renewable Energy (DREI) Sector,” were released in 2019. .
At the same time, the DREI report outlines cost-effective measures to boost investment opportunities in the four solar sub-sectors: first: large-scale solar energy; second: rooftop solar power; 3: Small grid system, solar and battery power; and 4: Home solar system.
The report also found that investing in more than 700 MW of solar energy could directly reduce CO2 emissions by 8.7 million tons by 2045. Myanmar regional MP shot dead in southern Burma
