In the speeches of world leaders, when referring to the political geography of the southern part of the world, most refer to the vast region called the "Asia-Pacific". The Asia-Pacific region is part of the world, or rather, it is adjacent to and close to the western Pacific Ocean, which generally includes East Asia, South Asia, Southeast Asia, and Oceania.
The term Asia Pacific can include parts of Russia (in the North Pacific Ocean) and some countries in the Americas along the eastern Pacific coast. When talking about the Asia-Pacific region, one also remembers the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC), which has 21 member countries, for example, Canada, Chile, Russia, Mexico, Peru and the United States.
On the other hand, the term is sometimes used to include all the countries in Australia when referring to the division of the world into large regions for commercial purposes.
The Indo-Pacific is a maritime confluence of the Indian Ocean and the Pacific Ocean. Many analysts see the Indo-Pacific region as a place for shifting power from the West to the East.
Since 2011, the term "Indo-Pacific" has been increasingly used in strategic / geopolitical discourses. As an international framework, the region seeks to establish a multilateral connectivity that must be governed by the norms of law and the application of generally agreed international norms.
