Taxi and tricycle drivers entering the Red Zone react by refusing to pay fines
(Phnom Penh): A group of taxi and tricycle drivers who came to pick up guests during the 19-strong Kovid outbreak in the Red Zone half a month ago reacted by refusing to pay fines for their crimes. The incident happened on the afternoon of May 26, 2021 at a fine location in Sangkat Choam Chao II, Khan Por Senchey, Phnom Penh.
The taxi and tricycle drivers were stopped half a month ago by Por Sen Chey district authorities as they were transporting people and vehicles on Veng Sreng Road in the red zone. After that, the police sent them to Chattaly Sak Center, Wat Kork Panchan Primary School, Sangkat Choam Chao II, and confiscated the car and tricycle and kept them in the field near the Sangkat Hall.
The taxi and tricycle drivers said they had finished their work and were allowed to return home. However, when retrieving cars and tricycles, the Por Senchey district police, under the direct command of Mr. Hem Darith, the district governor of Por Senchey and Mr. Mon Vuthy, the district police inspector, required them to pay fines in accordance with the law. In that, one car pays 2 million riels and one tricycle pays 1 million riels.
Taxi and tricycle riders say they have not been able to make money for half a month, so they have not been able to pay their fines.
However, in some cases, there was a negotiation, with some tricycle riders agreeing to pay only 300,000 riel and take the tricycle back home. Others have no money to go home alone, while the car or tricycle is kept by the authorities.
Regarding this case, Mr. Hem Darith pointed out that the taxi and tricycle drivers to transport people out of the red area is a serious violation of the law, which requires them to pay fines without concession.
