Human Rights Committee official: Civil society and opposition should work with government to fight Kovid rather than for prestige
(Phnom Penh): Chin Malin, spokesman for the Cambodian Human Rights Committee, criticized that in a democratic society, civil society and the opposition should join the government in the fight against Kovid 19, protect the lives of the people, rather than compete for the face of the people. Popularity and power for political gain.
Mr. Jean Malin said that in Cambodia, there are three types of civil society organizations, namely: civil society working for development, second: civil society working for political advocacy, and third: Civil society, hidden political activists and political activists under the guise of NGO associations.
At the same time, Mr. Jean Malin emphasized that civil society organizations established to serve the interests of society means to do all activities for the benefit of society and the interests of the people. Therefore, it means that civil society organizations are different from the private sector and political parties because civil society organizations are established not to engage in commercial activities for profit, but to act for the benefit of society and the people.
He added that another feature of the civil society was not to compete with the government or other political parties in order to gain popularity and support until it came to power. Up to do social work to protect the interests of the people ...!
Mr. Malin pointed out that the opposition is a non-governmental party called the opposition or opposition party to monitor the power race with the government to come to power through elections in the next term. In order to serve the interests of the people and society as a whole.
