(Kratie): Koh Sam Sip is a wonderful ecotourism community covering 3 villages, including Khsach Leav village, Koh Khnhe village and Poun Chea village of Prek Krieng commune, Sambo district, Kratie province. Spread along the Mekong River, this community is more than 90 kilometers from Kratie provincial town.
Floating on the surface of the Ganges, tourists will surely see the strange natural scenery that is waiting for a friendly welcome. Going forward, the ox carts of Cambodian farmers are ready to take tourists to visit the plantations on the island, which is full of green crops. Delicious food, fruit, sweet, cool, the smell of flowers in the fence drifting through the wind, delicious.
From the plantation, tourists continue on a ferry to one of the most special islands the community has prepared for the island, which you call the "Thirty Islands." The unique tourist treasures on the island are very rich, especially the camping sightseeing forest and the diverse species of birds that often coexist on the Mekong River.
As the treaty approaches, tourists will be able to unravel the complexities and frustrations with the beauty of the world, which is negotiating with the setting sun. When the rays of the sun disappear, it is time for the tourists to enjoy the delicious food of the countryside and enjoy the unforgettable atmosphere on the Thirty Islands. The view of relaxing in a tent under the moonlight at night has reached unimaginable serenity.
Early in the morning, Preah Sorya rode in a royal chariot from the East, the sound of birds singing and rejoicing to celebrate the new day, telling tourists not to miss the day after Nissai Kong met again.
Mr. Khun Sam Ol, President of Koh Samsip Ecotourism Community, said that the season when tourists can visit this community is all seasons, especially from October to June, which is the season when the island recedes from the beach. Clean sand, rich in fish and birds. But tourists can still visit between June and October, as tourists can take another tour.
In fact, the natural resources in the 50,093-hectare Sambo Wildlife Sanctuary all offer rich tourism potential for the community to use and provide the most attractive tourist services.
Mr. Khun Sam Ol continued that the community there is very satisfied with the service to serve tourists because this work gives them a satisfactory bonus that encourages them. He continues to work to preserve natural resource heritage and sustain the environment for a long time to come.
